---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeHash from '@/components/tools/security/DevEncodeHash.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="md5-hash-generator">
  <DevEncodeHash client:load algorithm="MD5" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What MD5 produces</h2>
    <p>
      An MD5 hash is a 128-bit digest, written as 32 hexadecimal characters, from an input of any
      length. The same input always gives the same digest, a one-byte change gives a completely
      different one, and you cannot rebuild the original data from it.
    </p>
    <pre><code>""              → d41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e
"hello"         → 5d41402abc4b2a76b9719d911017c592
"Hello"         → 8b1a9953c4611296a827abf8c47804d7</code></pre>
    <p>
      Learn the first value, the digest of the empty string. If a checksum comparison keeps giving it,
      your input is empty.
    </p>

    <h2>Broken for security, fine for corruption</h2>
    <p>
      MD5 collisions have been practical since 2004, and now take seconds to generate. An attacker who
      controls both files can give two different documents the same digest. So never use MD5 for
      anything an attacker can influence: passwords, signatures or deduplication of untrusted uploads.
    </p>
    <p>
      Accidental damage is a different problem. A truncated download, a bad disk sector or a corrupted
      transfer does not give a matching digest by chance: the odds are 1 in 2¹²⁸. So Linux
      distributions and vendor download pages still publish MD5 sums next to SHA-256 ones, and MD5 is
      correct for that purpose.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing by digest length</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Hex characters</th><th>Bits</th><th>Algorithm</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>8</td><td>32</td><td>CRC-32</td></tr>
          <tr><td>32</td><td>128</td><td>MD5</td></tr>
          <tr><td>40</td><td>160</td><td>SHA-1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>64</td><td>256</td><td>SHA-256</td></tr>
          <tr><td>128</td><td>512</td><td>SHA-512</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      When a checksum does not say which function made it, count the characters to identify it. The
      comparison table on this page shows all five for the same input.
    </p>

    <h2>Hashing large files</h2>
    <p>
      The tool computes MD5 as a stream: it reads the file in 4&nbsp;MB slices and each block updates
      the running state, so an 8&nbsp;GB disk image uses no more memory than a text file. Nothing is
      uploaded, so there is no size cap and no queue.
    </p>

    <h2>Why is MD5 not in WebCrypto?</h2>
    <p>
      The standard excludes obsolete algorithms on purpose. Browsers provide SHA-1 through SHA-512 and
      nothing weaker. So this page includes a compact pure-JavaScript MD5 implementation of RFC 1321: a
      few kilobytes that run in your tab with no network dependency.
    </p>

    <h2>Why your digest might not match someone else's</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A trailing newline: <code>echo "x" | md5sum</code> hashes two bytes, not one. Use <code>printf</code> or <code>echo -n</code>.</li>
      <li>Line endings: a file with CRLF has a different hash from the same file with LF. Git may have converted them on checkout.</li>
      <li>Encoding: the same text in UTF-8 and in UTF-16 is completely different bytes.</li>
      <li>A BOM: three invisible bytes at the start of a file change the hash.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
